WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.660
- [Narrator] Face it,
your house is filthy.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.061
It's filled with dead skin cells, dust,

00:00:06.061 --> 00:00:09.170
and around 200,000 different species

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:12.620
of bug, bacteria, and fungus.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.050
Now, it's tempting to assume that most

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.290
of them live in your toilet.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.270
After all it's where, you know,

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.990
but here's the thing,

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:25.510
the dirtiest place in your
house isn't your toilet.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.323
It's actually you.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:32.600
Household microbes lurk in
the most unlikely of places.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.400
Take a heat loving bacteria
called Thermus aquaticus,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.940
there guys can only be
found in two environments,

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.960
blisteringly hot geysers like
Old Faithful at Yellowstone

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.570
and your hot water heater.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.630
Another example is Penicillium fungi.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.970
They creep into your air
conditioner from outdoors

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.920
and when you switch on the AC

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.510
their spores get blasted around the house.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:00.190
They're known to cause
allergies in 2-6% of people.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:03.920
So when you notice a funny
musty smell coming from your AC

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.230
it's probably the fungus,

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:09.610
but before you start eyeing
your central air suspiciously,

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.900
think about this, you dump
microbes all over your body

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.790
every time you shower.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:20.400
It turns out trillions of
organisms live in your shower head

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.210
pilled on top of each
other in a slimy layer

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.320
half a millimeter thick,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.840
but that's not necessarily a problem.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.250
Researchers found that a type

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.200
of shower dwelling microbacterium

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.970
actually boosts levels of serotonin.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.680
That's a neurotransmitter
thought to lower stress

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.610
and increase happiness,

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.240
and when those arrant
microbacteria land on you

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.250
they join the tens of thousands
of other microbe species

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:48.070
that live in the dirtiest
place around, your body,

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:49.720
like Corynebacterium,

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.910
the microbes that give your
armpits that stinky odor

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.700
and simultaneously fight
off harmful pathogens

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.120
like E. Coli.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.930
Meanwhile, 300 to 500 species
of microbe colonize your gut

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:03.150
and are thought to play a role

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.190
in maintaining your immune system,

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.450
digestive system and even mental health.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.130
In the non bacteria category, microbes,

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.080
you've got such guests
as microscopic mites

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:15.500
that live on your face

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.650
and mole that colonizes into your toes.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.848
While the idea of them
might give you chills,

00:02:20.848 --> 00:02:24.200
microbes like these help keep you safe.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.480
You see the number of

00:02:26.469 --> 00:02:27.840
harmless microbial species in the world

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.360
outnumber harmful ones,
by a trillion to one.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.340
In fact, less than 0,00000001%
of microbial species

00:02:38.077 --> 00:02:43.020
account for nearly all
infectious diseases in the world

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:46.000
and all these harmless
bacteria crawling on your skin

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.810
means less room and
resources for pathogens

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.590
like antibiotic resistant microbes,

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.810
which also means they
have less opportunity

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.630
to take over and make you sick.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:59.330
So even when antibiotics fail,

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:02.060
it could be all those
other microbes crawling in

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.670
and on you that keep you safe.

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:08.720
So in a way, you need to get a
little dirty to stay healthy,

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:09.860
but full disclosure,

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.210
that's not an excuse to
swear off your shower.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.020
Besides, you don't wanna miss out

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:17.088
on that feel good microbacterium.

00:03:17.088 --> 00:03:21.150
This video was made in large
part thanks to Rob Dunn

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.863
and the information in his
new book Never Home Alone.

